item.description:
It is always fun to speculate. Although, a few things didn&#039;t mesh completely for me...

&lt;blockquote&gt;If you drop a ball from rest (defined to be a state of zero energy), it gains energy of motion (kinetic energy) as it falls. But this gain is exactly balanced by a larger negative gravitational energy as it comes closer to Earth’s center, so the sum of the two energies remains zero.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Is this assuming the earth is an empty space in which the ball can pass through to the center, yet have mass that attracts the ball? An negative gravitational energy? I suppose that&#039;s a reasonable label for framing certain points of view. The problem with this can be we end up counting angels on the head of pins. That&#039;s okay too, of course. I guess I&#039;m too lazy to engage in so much counting.

&lt;blockquote&gt;It might be the vacuum in some pre-existing space and time, or it could be nothing at all – that is, all concepts of space and time were created with the universe itself.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

The &#039;Nothing&#039; of the taoist point of view is not a vacuum or space. Those are metaphors for &lt;em&gt;true nothing&lt;/em&gt;. Naturally, even giving a name for this &#039;nothing&#039; is a misrepresentation. Alas,... names and words... :roll:
